{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggyXwvLBXq1YB7fiF8YL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012DqTqDTo8pPYZap7bgYg3z","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Wolf","prompt":"Summarize the arcade game's gameplay in detail: the protagonist's name, the mission/story, the six stages in order (and what completing each stage does, e.g. restoring health or ammo), the HUD counters (enemy quotas: soldiers, helicopters, vehicles/boats), weapons (magazines, bullets, grenades/rockets), how ammo and reloading work, what happens when ammo runs out, the damage meter, power-ups/items (magazines, grenades, energy drink, free machine gun etc.) and how they are obtained, civilians/hostages/nurses and the penalty for shooting them, enemy types (soldiers, knife throwers, tanks, armoured cars, helicopters, gunboats), the ending and how it depends on hostages saved, the cabinet's gun (recoil), the release year, developer, and the sequels. Quote specific numbers where given."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3719,"cache_read_input_tokens":149888,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3719},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d128962a-bd2b-4da0-bea3-10df26a8e825","uuid":"def6dc3b-383e-4abb-a6bf-bf96acb130fa","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:54:29.443Z","request_id":"req_011CggyXwfTvogGxiuXYSCbD","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012DqTqDTo8pPYZap7bgYg3z":{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Wolf","prompt":"Summarize the arcade game's gameplay in detail: the protagonist's name, the mission/story, the six stages in order (and what completing each stage does, e.g. restoring health or ammo), the HUD counters (enemy quotas: soldiers, helicopters, vehicles/boats), weapons (magazines, bullets, grenades/rockets), how ammo and reloading work, what happens when ammo runs out, the damage meter, power-ups/items (magazines, grenades, energy drink, free machine gun etc.) and how they are obtained, civilians/hostages/nurses and the penalty for shooting them, enemy types (soldiers, knife throwers, tanks, armoured cars, helicopters, gunboats), the ending and how it depends on hostages saved, the cabinet's gun (recoil), the release year, developer, and the sequels. Quote specific numbers where given."}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012DqTqDTo8pPYZap7bgYg3z","type":"tool_result","content":"**Operation Wolf** (Taito, 1987) is a first-person light gun rail shooter for arcades. The page doesn't cover many of the details you asked for, so I've marked those gaps.\n\n**Story and structure**\n- The protagonist is Special Forces operative Roy Adams.\n- His mission is to rescue five hostages held in enemy territory.\n- The game has six stages that advance the story. After the jungle stage, Adams interrogates an enemy soldier, who reveals the location of a concentration camp and the hostages.\n- Each stage has its own objectives and effects once completed, all tied to rescuing hostages. The page doesn't say what each stage's completion does, such as restoring health or ammo.\n- Game-over screens vary. One occurs when the player dies, another when a single hostage is not rescued. Continuing restarts the current stage.\n- The NES version has multiple endings based on the number of hostages rescued. The page doesn't describe the other versions' endings.\n\n**Controls and weapon**\n- The arcade cabinet's gun resembles an Uzi. The player can swivel and elevate it, and it vibrates to simulate gunfire recoil.\n- Pulling the trigger fires fully automatic.\n- A button near the muzzle launches a grenade with a wide blast radius that can hit multiple targets.\n\n**Objectives and HUD**\n- Each stage requires shooting a set number of soldiers and vehicles, including trucks, boats, helicopters, and armored transports. An on-screen counter tracks progress. The page gives no specific quota numbers.\n\n**Ammo, grenades, and power-ups**\n- Ammo and grenades are limited and can be replenished by shooting objects. The page doesn't say which objects, and it gives no magazine or bullet counts.\n- Shooting dynamite bombs damages every target on screen, enemy and friendly alike.\n- A special machine gun power-up gives unlimited ammo and a faster rate of fire for 10 seconds.\n- The page doesn't mention an energy drink or how power-ups are otherwise obtained.\n\n**Enemies and projectiles**\n- Enemies attack with gunfire, knives, grenades, mortar and bazooka rounds, and missiles. The player can shoot down all visible incoming projectiles.\n- The page doesn't describe specific enemy types such as knife throwers, tanks, armored cars, or gunboats, beyond the vehicle targets listed above.\n\n**Damage meter and civilians**\n- A damage bar fills when enemies hit the player or when the player shoots friendly targets, which the page lists as nurses and boys.\n- Damage is recovered by collecting health power-ups and by completing stages.\n\n**Release and developer**\n- Developer and publisher: Taito.\n- Arcade release: October 1987 in Japan and Europe, December 1987 in North America.\n- Home ports followed on many systems, including the NES, Master System, ZX Spectrum, Amiga, and others.\n\n**Sequels and related releases**\n- *Operation Thunderbolt* (1988)\n- *Operation Wolf 3* (1994)\n- *Operation Tiger* (1998)\n- *Operation Wolf Returns: First Mission* (2023), a remake"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d128962a-bd2b-4da0-bea3-10df26a8e825","uuid":"aba3b967-dd6c-462a-b97d-33dbae67978d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:54:42.694Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":291596,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Operation Wolf** (Taito, 1987) is a first-person light gun rail shooter for arcades. The page doesn't cover many of the details you asked for, so I've marked those gaps.\n\n**Story and structure**\n- The protagonist is Special Forces operative Roy Adams.\n- His mission is to rescue five hostages held in enemy territory.\n- The game has six stages that advance the story. After the jungle stage, Adams interrogates an enemy soldier, who reveals the location of a concentration camp and the hostages.\n- Each stage has its own objectives and effects once completed, all tied to rescuing hostages. The page doesn't say what each stage's completion does, such as restoring health or ammo.\n- Game-over screens vary. One occurs when the player dies, another when a single hostage is not rescued. Continuing restarts the current stage.\n- The NES version has multiple endings based on the number of hostages rescued. The page doesn't describe the other versions' endings.\n\n**Controls and weapon**\n- The arcade cabinet's gun resembles an Uzi. The player can swivel and elevate it, and it vibrates to simulate gunfire recoil.\n- Pulling the trigger fires fully automatic.\n- A button near the muzzle launches a grenade with a wide blast radius that can hit multiple targets.\n\n**Objectives and HUD**\n- Each stage requires shooting a set number of soldiers and vehicles, including trucks, boats, helicopters, and armored transports. An on-screen counter tracks progress. The page gives no specific quota numbers.\n\n**Ammo, grenades, and power-ups**\n- Ammo and grenades are limited and can be replenished by shooting objects. The page doesn't say which objects, and it gives no magazine or bullet counts.\n- Shooting dynamite bombs damages every target on screen, enemy and friendly alike.\n- A special machine gun power-up gives unlimited ammo and a faster rate of fire for 10 seconds.\n- The page doesn't mention an energy drink or how power-ups are otherwise obtained.\n\n**Enemies and projectiles**\n- Enemies attack with gunfire, knives, grenades, mortar and bazooka rounds, and missiles. The player can shoot down all visible incoming projectiles.\n- The page doesn't describe specific enemy types such as knife throwers, tanks, armored cars, or gunboats, beyond the vehicle targets listed above.\n\n**Damage meter and civilians**\n- A damage bar fills when enemies hit the player or when the player shoots friendly targets, which the page lists as nurses and boys.\n- Damage is recovered by collecting health power-ups and by completing stages.\n\n**Release and developer**\n- Developer and publisher: Taito.\n- Arcade release: October 1987 in Japan and Europe, December 1987 in North America.\n- Home ports followed on many systems, including the NES, Master System, ZX Spectrum, Amiga, and others.\n\n**Sequels and related releases**\n- *Operation Thunderbolt* (1988)\n- *Operation Wolf 3* (1994)\n- *Operation Tiger* (1998)\n- *Operation Wolf Returns: First Mission* (2023), a remake","durationMs":13246,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Wolf"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012DqTqDTo8pPYZap7bgYg3z","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
