{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggyandpSqZvZ9uBegsRu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016NMLKfwJ58cqXHdNe8x2zf","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://www.arcade-history.com/game/1887/","prompt":"Extract all gameplay details about the arcade Operation Wolf: story text, stage list and what completing each stage does (bonuses to later stages: e.g. fewer enemies, health restored, ammo restored), items/power-ups and how they're obtained, enemy types, civilians and penalties, hostages, the ending and ranking, HUD elements, the cabinet's gun and recoil, music/sound notes, and any trivia (e.g. the main character's name, the interrogation scene). Give specifics."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":623,"cache_read_input_tokens":156564,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":623},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d128962a-bd2b-4da0-bea3-10df26a8e825","uuid":"470b7b3d-111a-401b-8e6d-fcd2b6ed3032","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:55:08.146Z","request_id":"req_011CggyanRRhRmnvaHbbBTEp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016NMLKfwJ58cqXHdNe8x2zf":{"url":"https://www.arcade-history.com/game/1887/","prompt":"Extract all gameplay details about the arcade Operation Wolf: story text, stage list and what completing each stage does (bonuses to later stages: e.g. fewer enemies, health restored, ammo restored), items/power-ups and how they're obtained, enemy types, civilians and penalties, hostages, the ending and ranking, HUD elements, the cabinet's gun and recoil, music/sound notes, and any trivia (e.g. the main character's name, the interrogation scene). Give specifics."}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016NMLKfwJ58cqXHdNe8x2zf","type":"tool_result","content":"**Story:** You play Special Forces operative 'Roy Adams', who must infiltrate and capture six heavily fortified bases and rescue five trapped hostages.\n\n**Stages (in order):** Communication Setup, Jungle, Village, Powder Magazine, Concentration Camp, Airport.\n\n**Stage rewards and effects:**\n- **Powder Magazine:** Completing it refills ammo and grenades to maximum.\n- **Village:** Completing it restores a large amount of damage (the tips say 20 points).\n- **Other stages:** Completing each restores a small amount (the tips say 3 points).\n- **Airport:** A bonus is awarded based on how many hostages board the plane.\n- The page does not say that clearing stages reduces enemy counts in later stages.\n\n**Enemies:**\n- Soldiers, who throw hand grenades and knives.\n- Vehicles, which fire missiles and launch rockets.\n- Helicopters, which can be shot down for points. The tips say they take many hits to destroy.\n- Large blond soldiers with machine guns, who go down quickly if shot in the face (tips).\n- Mortar crews behind sandbags (tips).\n\n**Civilians and hostages:**\n- Shooting a civilian or hostage increases the damage bar.\n- Hostages appear in the Concentration Camp stage, where the player must protect them while they run to safety.\n- In the Airport stage, hostages run toward an airplane hatch while enemies attack.\n\n**Items and power-ups:**\n- **Ammo and grenades:** Obtained by shooting barrels and crates. Both are limited.\n- **High-powered machine gun:** Occasionally appears. It gives unlimited ammo and a faster rate of fire for 10 seconds.\n- **Energy boost:** Appears randomly. Shooting it reduces damage by five points.\n- **Power drinks:** Mentioned in the tips as items that can appear.\n- **Farm animals:** Chickens and pigs occasionally cross the screen. Shooting them reveals bonuses such as health or ammo.\n\n**Penalties:** Getting hit, or shooting a civilian or hostage, raises the damage bar.\n\n**Game over:** The game ends if the damage bar fills completely or if you run out of both ammo and grenades.\n\n**Progression:** After completing the game, you return to the first mission at a higher difficulty.\n\n**HUD:** A damage bar appears to the right of the main play screen.\n\n**Ending and ranking:** The page does not describe an ending sequence or a ranking system. It lists Mark Twitty as the official record holder with 212,350 points.\n\n**Cabinet and controls:** One player, a lightgun control, and two buttons. The page does not mention gun recoil.\n\n**Music and sound:**\n- Music by Hiroshi Tuda.\n- Sound chips: Yamaha YM2151 and two OKI MSM5205 chips.\n- Pony Canyon / Scitron released a limited-edition soundtrack album on June 21, 1988.\n\n**Trivia:**\n- Released November 1987; developed by East Technology.\n- A bootleg is known as Operation Bear.\n- The page does not mention an interrogation scene.\n\n**Discrepancies:** The tips refer to the \"Ammo Dump\" as stage four and the first stage as \"enemy radar,\" while the main text names the stages Powder Magazine and Communication Setup."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d128962a-bd2b-4da0-bea3-10df26a8e825","uuid":"85be676f-e053-4a73-b1fa-579b7f562da9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:55:20.928Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":63256,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Story:** You play Special Forces operative 'Roy Adams', who must infiltrate and capture six heavily fortified bases and rescue five trapped hostages.\n\n**Stages (in order):** Communication Setup, Jungle, Village, Powder Magazine, Concentration Camp, Airport.\n\n**Stage rewards and effects:**\n- **Powder Magazine:** Completing it refills ammo and grenades to maximum.\n- **Village:** Completing it restores a large amount of damage (the tips say 20 points).\n- **Other stages:** Completing each restores a small amount (the tips say 3 points).\n- **Airport:** A bonus is awarded based on how many hostages board the plane.\n- The page does not say that clearing stages reduces enemy counts in later stages.\n\n**Enemies:**\n- Soldiers, who throw hand grenades and knives.\n- Vehicles, which fire missiles and launch rockets.\n- Helicopters, which can be shot down for points. The tips say they take many hits to destroy.\n- Large blond soldiers with machine guns, who go down quickly if shot in the face (tips).\n- Mortar crews behind sandbags (tips).\n\n**Civilians and hostages:**\n- Shooting a civilian or hostage increases the damage bar.\n- Hostages appear in the Concentration Camp stage, where the player must protect them while they run to safety.\n- In the Airport stage, hostages run toward an airplane hatch while enemies attack.\n\n**Items and power-ups:**\n- **Ammo and grenades:** Obtained by shooting barrels and crates. Both are limited.\n- **High-powered machine gun:** Occasionally appears. It gives unlimited ammo and a faster rate of fire for 10 seconds.\n- **Energy boost:** Appears randomly. Shooting it reduces damage by five points.\n- **Power drinks:** Mentioned in the tips as items that can appear.\n- **Farm animals:** Chickens and pigs occasionally cross the screen. Shooting them reveals bonuses such as health or ammo.\n\n**Penalties:** Getting hit, or shooting a civilian or hostage, raises the damage bar.\n\n**Game over:** The game ends if the damage bar fills completely or if you run out of both ammo and grenades.\n\n**Progression:** After completing the game, you return to the first mission at a higher difficulty.\n\n**HUD:** A damage bar appears to the right of the main play screen.\n\n**Ending and ranking:** The page does not describe an ending sequence or a ranking system. It lists Mark Twitty as the official record holder with 212,350 points.\n\n**Cabinet and controls:** One player, a lightgun control, and two buttons. The page does not mention gun recoil.\n\n**Music and sound:**\n- Music by Hiroshi Tuda.\n- Sound chips: Yamaha YM2151 and two OKI MSM5205 chips.\n- Pony Canyon / Scitron released a limited-edition soundtrack album on June 21, 1988.\n\n**Trivia:**\n- Released November 1987; developed by East Technology.\n- A bootleg is known as Operation Bear.\n- The page does not mention an interrogation scene.\n\n**Discrepancies:** The tips refer to the \"Ammo Dump\" as stage four and the first stage as \"enemy radar,\" while the main text names the stages Powder Magazine and Communication Setup.","durationMs":12779,"url":"https://www.arcade-history.com/game/1887/"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016NMLKfwJ58cqXHdNe8x2zf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
